#. type: Title #
#, no-wrap
msgid "The phase estimation problem {#the-phase-estimation-problem}"
msgstr "El problema de estimación de fase {#the-phase-estimation-problem}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This section of the lesson explains the *phase estimation problem*.\n"
"We'll begin with a short discussion of the *spectral theorem* from linear algebra, and then move on to a statement of the phase estimation problem itself.\n"
msgstr ""
"Esta sección de la lección explica el *problema de estimación de fase*.\n"
"Comenzamos con una breve discusión del *teorema espectral* de álgebra lineal y luego pasamos a la formulación del problema de estimación de fase en sí.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spectral theorem {#spectral-theorem}"
msgstr "Teorema espectral {#spectral-theorem}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The *spectral theorem* is an important fact from linear algebra that states that matrices of a certain type, called *normal matrices,* can be expressed in a simple and useful way.\n"
"We'll only need this theorem for unitary matrices in this lesson, but down the road in this series we'll apply it to Hermitian matrices as well.\n"
msgstr ""
"El *teorema espectral* es un resultado importante del álgebra lineal que establece que las matrices de cierto tipo — llamadas *matrices normales* — pueden representarse de una forma simple y útil.\n"
"Necesitamos este teorema en esta lección solo para matrices unitarias, pero más adelante en esta serie también lo aplicaremos a matrices hermitianas.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Normal matrices {#normal-matrices}"
msgstr "Matrices normales {#normal-matrices}"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A square matrix $M$ with complex number entries is said to be a *normal* matrix if it commutes with its conjugate transpose:\n"
"$M M^{\\dagger} = M^{\\dagger} M$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Una matriz cuadrada $M$ con entradas complejas se denomina *matriz normal* si conmuta con su conjugada traspuesta:\n"
"$M M^{\\dagger} = M^{\\dagger} M.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Every unitary matrix $U$ is normal because\n"
msgstr "Toda matriz unitaria $U$ es normal, porque\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"U U^{\\dagger} = \\mathbb{I} = U^{\\dagger} U.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"U U^{\\dagger} = \\mathbb{I} = U^{\\dagger} U.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Hermitian matrices, which are matrices that equal their own conjugate transpose, are another important class of normal matrices.\n"
"If $H$ is a Hermitian matrix, then\n"
msgstr ""
"Las matrices hermitianas, es decir, matrices que son iguales a su propia conjugada traspuesta, son otra clase importante de matrices normales.\n"
"Si $H$ es una matriz hermitiana, entonces\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H H^{\\dagger} = H^2 = H^{\\dagger} H,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"H H^{\\dagger} = H^2 = H^{\\dagger} H,\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "so $H$ is normal.\n"
msgstr "por lo que $H$ es normal.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Not every square matrix is normal.\n"
"For instance, this matrix isn't normal:\n"
msgstr ""
"No toda matriz cuadrada es normal.\n"
"Por ejemplo, la siguiente matriz no es normal:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(This is a simple but great example of a matrix that's often very helpful to consider.)\n"
"It isn't normal because\n"
msgstr ""
"(Este es un ejemplo simple pero excelente de una matriz que a menudo resulta muy útil de considerar.)\n"
"No es normal porque\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}^{\\dagger}\n"
"=\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}^{\\dagger}\n"
"=\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 0\\\\\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 0\\\\\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "while\n"
msgstr "mientras que\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}^{\\dagger}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}^{\\dagger}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 0\\\\\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 0\\\\\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 0\\\\\n"
"0 & 1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 0\\\\\n"
"0 & 1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Theorem statement {#theorem-statement}"
msgstr "Enunciado del teorema {#theorem-statement}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now here's a statement of the spectral theorem.\n"
msgstr "Aquí hay un enunciado del teorema espectral.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Figure title=\"Theorem\">\n"
"Spectral theorem: Let $M$ be a normal $N\\times N$ complex matrix.\n"
"There exists an orthonormal basis of $N$-dimensional complex vectors $\\bigl\\{ \\vert\\psi_1\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_N\\rangle \\bigr\\}$ along with complex numbers $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_N$ such that\n"
"$$\n"
"M = \\lambda_1 \\vert \\psi_1\\rangle\\langle \\psi_1\\vert + \\cdots + \\lambda_N \\vert \\psi_N\\rangle\\langle \\psi_N\\vert.\n"
"$$\n"
"</Figure>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Figure title=\"Teorema\">\n"
"Teorema espectral: Sea $M$ una matriz compleja normal de $N\\times N$.\n"
"Existe una base ortonormal de vectores complejos $N$-dimensionales $\\bigl\\{ \\vert\\psi_1\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_N\\rangle \\bigr\\}$ y números complejos $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_N$ tales que\n"
"$$\n"
"M = \\lambda_1 \\vert \\psi_1\\rangle\\langle \\psi_1\\vert + \\cdots + \\lambda_N \\vert \\psi_N\\rangle\\langle \\psi_N\\vert.\n"
"$$\n"
"</Figure>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The expression of a matrix in the form\n"
msgstr "La representación de una matriz en la forma\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"M = \\sum_{k = 1}^N \\lambda_k \\vert \\psi_k\\rangle\\langle \\psi_k\\vert \\tag{1}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"M = \\sum_{k = 1}^N \\lambda_k \\vert \\psi_k\\rangle\\langle \\psi_k\\vert \\tag{1}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"is commonly called a *spectral decomposition*.\n"
"Notice that if $M$ is a normal matrix expressed in the form $(1)$, then the equation\n"
msgstr ""
"se conoce generalmente como *descomposición espectral*.\n"
"Observa: si $M$ es una matriz normal expresada en la forma $(1)$, entonces la ecuación\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"M \\vert \\psi_j \\rangle = \\lambda_j \\vert \\psi_j \\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"M \\vert \\psi_j \\rangle = \\lambda_j \\vert \\psi_j \\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"must be true for every $j = 1,\\ldots,N$.\n"
"This is a consequence of the fact that $\\bigl\\{ \\vert\\psi_1\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_N\\rangle \\bigr\\}$ is orthonormal:\n"
msgstr ""
"debe cumplirse para cada $j = 1,\\ldots,N$.\n"
"Esto se deduce del hecho de que $\\bigl\\{ \\vert\\psi_1\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_N\\rangle \\bigr\\}$ es ortonormal:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"M \\vert \\psi_j \\rangle\n"
"= \\left(\\sum_{k = 1}^N \\lambda_k \\vert \\psi_k\\rangle\\langle \\psi_k\\vert\\right)\\vert \\psi_j\\rangle\n"
"= \\sum_{k = 1}^N \\lambda_k \\vert \\psi_k\\rangle\\langle \\psi_k\\vert\\psi_j \\rangle\n"
"= \\lambda_j \\vert\\psi_j \\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"M \\vert \\psi_j \\rangle\n"
"= \\left(\\sum_{k = 1}^N \\lambda_k \\vert \\psi_k\\rangle\\langle \\psi_k\\vert\\right)\\vert \\psi_j\\rangle\n"
"= \\sum_{k = 1}^N \\lambda_k \\vert \\psi_k\\rangle\\langle \\psi_k\\vert\\psi_j \\rangle\n"
"= \\lambda_j \\vert\\psi_j \\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "That is, each number $\\lambda_j$ is an *eigenvalue* of $M$ and $\\vert\\psi_j\\rangle$ is an *eigenvector* corresponding to that eigenvalue.\n"
msgstr "Es decir, cada número $\\lambda_j$ es un *valor propio* de $M$ y $\\vert\\psi_j\\rangle$ es un *vector propio* correspondiente a ese valor propio.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Example 1**.\n"
"Let\n"
"$$\n"
"\\mathbb{I} = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\\\0 & 1\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"**Ejemplo 1**.\n"
"Sea\n"
"$$\n"
"\\mathbb{I} = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\\\0 & 1\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  which is normal.\n"
"  The theorem implies that $\\mathbb{I}$ can be written in the form $(1)$ for some choice of $\\lambda_1$, $\\lambda_2$, $\\vert\\psi_1\\rangle$, and $\\vert\\psi_2\\rangle$.\n"
"  There are multiple choices that work, including\n"
msgstr ""
"  que es normal.\n"
"  El teorema implica que $\\mathbb{I}$ puede escribirse en la forma $(1)$ para una elección adecuada de $\\lambda_1,$ $\\lambda_2,$ $\\vert\\psi_1\\rangle$ y $\\vert\\psi_2\\rangle$.\n"
"  Hay varias posibilidades, incluyendo\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\lambda_1 = 1, \\hspace{5pt}\n"
"  \\lambda_2 = 1, \\hspace{5pt}\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_1\\rangle = \\vert 0\\rangle, \\hspace{5pt}\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_2\\rangle = \\vert 1\\rangle.\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\lambda_1 = 1, \\hspace{5pt}\n"
"  \\lambda_2 = 1, \\hspace{5pt}\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_1\\rangle = \\vert 0\\rangle, \\hspace{5pt}\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_2\\rangle = \\vert 1\\rangle.\n"
"  $$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Notice that the theorem does not say that the complex numbers $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_N$ are\n"
"  distinct — we can have the same complex number repeated, which is necessary for this example.\n"
"  These choices work because\n"
"  $$\n"
"  \\mathbb{I} = \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert + \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert.\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""
"  Observa que el teorema no dice que los números complejos $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_N$ deban ser distintos — el mismo número complejo puede aparecer más de una vez, lo cual es necesario en este ejemplo.\n"
"  Esta elección funciona porque\n"
"  $$\n"
"  \\mathbb{I} = \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert + \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert.\n"
"  $$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Indeed, we could choose $\\{\\vert\\psi_1\\rangle,\\vert\\psi_2\\rangle\\}$ to be *any* orthonormal basis and the\n"
"  equation will be true. For instance,\n"
msgstr "  De hecho, podríamos elegir $\\{\\vert\\psi_1\\rangle,\\vert\\psi_2\\rangle\\}$ como *cualquier* base ortonormal y la ecuación se cumpliría. Por ejemplo:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\mathbb{I} = \\vert +\\rangle\\langle +\\vert + \\vert -\\rangle\\langle -\\vert.\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\mathbb{I} = \\vert +\\rangle\\langle +\\vert + \\vert -\\rangle\\langle -\\vert.\n"
"  $$\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Example 2**.\n"
"Consider a Hadamard operation.\n"
msgstr ""
"**Ejemplo 2**.\n"
"Consideremos una operación de Hadamard.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  H = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  1 & 1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"  1 & -1\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""
"  $$\n"
"  H = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  1 & 1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"  1 & -1\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"  $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  This is a unitary matrix, so it is normal. The spectral theorem implies that $H$ can be written in the\n"
"  form $(1)$, and in particular we have\n"
msgstr "  Es una matriz unitaria, así que es normal. El teorema espectral implica que $H$ puede escribirse en la forma $(1)$, y en particular\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  H =\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{\\pi/8}\\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"  - \\vert\\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\rangle \\langle \\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""
"  $$\n"
"  H =\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{\\pi/8}\\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"  - \\vert\\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\rangle \\langle \\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"  $$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  where\n"
msgstr "  donde\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{\\theta}\\rangle\n"
"  = \\cos(\\theta)\\vert 0\\rangle + \\sin(\\theta) \\vert 1\\rangle.\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{\\theta}\\rangle\n"
"  = \\cos(\\theta)\\vert 0\\rangle + \\sin(\\theta) \\vert 1\\rangle.\n"
"  $$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  More explicitly,\n"
msgstr "  Más explícitamente:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\begin{aligned}\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{\\pi/8}\\rangle & = \\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 0\\rangle\n"
"  + \\frac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 1\\rangle, \\\\[3mm]\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\rangle & = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 0\\rangle\n"
"  + \\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 1\\rangle.\n"
"  \\end{aligned}\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\begin{aligned}\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{\\pi/8}\\rangle & = \\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 0\\rangle\n"
"  + \\frac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 1\\rangle, \\\\[3mm]\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\rangle & = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 0\\rangle\n"
"  + \\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 1\\rangle.\n"
"  \\end{aligned}\n"
"  $$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  We can check that this decomposition is correct by performing the required calculations:\n"
msgstr "  Podemos verificar que esta descomposición es correcta realizando los cálculos necesarios:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{\\pi/8}\\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"  - \\vert\\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\rangle \\langle \\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"  = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"  \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"  -\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  \\frac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{4} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"  -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"  = H.\n"
"  $$\n"
msgstr ""
"  $$\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{\\pi/8}\\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"  - \\vert\\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\rangle \\langle \\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\vert\n"
"  = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"  \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"  -\n"
"  \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  \\frac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{4} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"  -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n"
"  \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"  = H.\n"
"  $$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As the first example above reveals, there can be some freedom in how eigenvectors are selected.\n"
"There is, however, no freedom at all in how the eigenvalues are chosen, except for their ordering:\n"
"the same $N$ complex numbers $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_N$, which can include repetitions of the same complex number, will always occur in the equation $(1)$ for a given choice of a matrix $M$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Como muestra el primer ejemplo anterior, puede haber cierta libertad en la elección de los vectores propios.\n"
"En la elección de los valores propios, sin embargo, no hay ninguna libertad, salvo su orden: para una matriz dada $M$, siempre aparecen los mismos $N$ números complejos $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_N$ en la ecuación $(1)$, que pueden incluir repeticiones del mismo número complejo.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now let's focus in on unitary matrices.\n"
"Suppose we have a complex number $\\lambda$ and a non-zero vector $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ that satisfy the equation\n"
msgstr ""
"Centrémonos ahora en las matrices unitarias.\n"
"Supongamos que tenemos un número complejo $\\lambda$ y un vector no nulo $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ que satisfacen la ecuación\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"U\\vert\\psi\\rangle = \\lambda\\vert\\psi\\rangle.\n"
"\\tag{2}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"U\\vert\\psi\\rangle = \\lambda\\vert\\psi\\rangle.\n"
"\\tag{2}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "That is, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $U$ and $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ is an eigenvector corresponding to this eigenvalue.\n"
msgstr "Es decir, $\\lambda$ es un valor propio de $U$ y $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ es un vector propio correspondiente a ese valor propio.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unitary matrices preserve Euclidean norm, and so we conclude the following from $(2)$.\n"
msgstr "Las matrices unitarias preservan la norma euclídea, y por lo tanto concluimos de $(2)$ lo siguiente.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\bigl\\| \\vert\\psi\\rangle \\bigr\\|\n"
"= \\bigl\\| U \\vert\\psi\\rangle \\bigr\\|\n"
"= \\bigl\\| \\lambda \\vert\\psi\\rangle \\bigr\\|\n"
"= \\vert \\lambda \\vert \\bigl\\| \\vert\\psi\\rangle \\bigr\\|\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\bigl\\| \\vert\\psi\\rangle \\bigr\\|\n"
"= \\bigl\\| U \\vert\\psi\\rangle \\bigr\\|\n"
"= \\bigl\\| \\lambda \\vert\\psi\\rangle \\bigr\\|\n"
"= \\vert \\lambda \\vert \\bigl\\| \\vert\\psi\\rangle \\bigr\\|\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The condition that $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ is non-zero implies that $\\bigl\\| \\vert\\psi\\rangle \\bigr\\|\\neq 0$, so we can cancel it from both sides to obtain\n"
msgstr "La condición de que $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ no sea nulo implica que $\\bigl\\| \\vert\\psi\\rangle \\bigr\\|\\neq 0$, por lo que podemos simplificar ambos lados y obtener:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\lambda \\vert = 1.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\lambda \\vert = 1.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This reveals that eigenvalues of unitary matrices must always have absolute value equal to one, so they lie on the *unit circle.*\n"
msgstr "Esto muestra que los valores propios de matrices unitarias siempre tienen módulo uno y, por lo tanto, se encuentran en el *círculo unitario*.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\mathbb{T} = \\{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{C} : \\vert\\alpha\\vert = 1\\}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\mathbb{T} = \\{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{C} : \\vert\\alpha\\vert = 1\\}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(The symbol $\\mathbb{T}$ is a common name for the complex unit circle. The name $S^1$ is also common.)\n"
msgstr "(El símbolo $\\mathbb{T}$ es un nombre común para el círculo unitario complejo. El nombre $S^1$ también es común.)\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Phase estimation problem statement {#phase-estimation-problem-statement}"
msgstr "Enunciado del problema de estimación de fase {#phase-estimation-problem-statement}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the *phase estimation problem*, we're given a quantum state $\\vert \\psi\\rangle$ of $n$ qubits, along with a unitary quantum circuit that acts on $n$ qubits.\n"
"We're *promised* that $\\vert \\psi\\rangle$ is an eigenvector of the unitary matrix $U$ that describes the action of the circuit, and our goal is to compute or approximate the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ to which $\\vert \\psi\\rangle$ corresponds.\n"
"More precisely, because $\\lambda$ lies on the complex unit circle, we can write\n"
msgstr ""
"En el *problema de estimación de fase*, se da un estado cuántico $\\vert \\psi\\rangle$ de $n$ qubits junto con un circuito cuántico unitario que actúa sobre $n$ qubits.\n"
"Se *promete* que $\\vert \\psi\\rangle$ es un vector propio de la matriz unitaria $U$ que describe la acción del circuito, y nuestro objetivo es calcular o aproximar el valor propio $\\lambda$ correspondiente a $\\vert \\psi\\rangle$.\n"
"Como $\\lambda$ está en el círculo unitario complejo, podemos escribir\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\lambda = e^{2\\pi i \\theta}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\lambda = e^{2\\pi i \\theta}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for a unique real number $\\theta$ satisfying $0\\leq\\theta<1$.\n"
"The goal of the problem is to compute or approximate this real number $\\theta$.\n"
msgstr ""
"para un número real único $\\theta$ con $0\\leq\\theta<1$.\n"
"El objetivo del problema es calcular o aproximar este número real $\\theta$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Figure title=\"Phase estimation problem\">\n"
"Input: A unitary quantum circuit for an $n$-qubit operation $U$ along with an $n$-qubit quantum state $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ \\\n"
"Promise: $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ is an eigenvector of $U$ \\\n"
"Output: an approximation to the number $\\theta\\in[0,1)$ satisfying $U\\vert\\psi\\rangle = e^{2\\pi i \\theta}\\vert\\psi\\rangle$\n"
"</Figure>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Figure title=\"Problema de estimación de fase\">\n"
"Entrada: Un circuito cuántico unitario para una operación de $n$ qubits $U$ y un estado cuántico de $n$ qubits $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ \\\n"
"Promesa: $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ es un vector propio de $U$ \\\n"
"Salida: una aproximación del número $\\theta\\in[0,1)$ con $U\\vert\\psi\\rangle = e^{2\\pi i \\theta}\\vert\\psi\\rangle$\n"
"</Figure>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here are a few remarks about this problem statement:\n"
msgstr "Aquí hay algunas observaciones sobre esta formulación del problema:\n"

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The phase estimation problem is different from other problems we've seen so far in the course in that the input includes a quantum state. Typically we focus on problems having classical inputs and outputs, but nothing prevents us from considering quantum state inputs like this. In terms of its practical relevance, the phase estimation problem is typically encountered as a *subproblem* inside of a larger computation, like we'll see in the context of integer factorization later in the lesson.\n"
msgstr "El problema de estimación de fase difiere de otros problemas tratados hasta ahora en el curso en que la entrada incluye un estado cuántico. Normalmente nos centramos en problemas con entradas y salidas clásicas, pero nada impide considerar estados cuánticos como entradas. En cuanto a su relevancia práctica, el problema de estimación de fase típicamente aparece como un *subproblema* dentro de un cálculo mayor, como veremos más adelante en la lección en el contexto de la factorización de enteros.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The statement of the phase estimation problem above isn't specific about what constitutes an approximation of $\\theta$, but we can formulate more precise problem statements depending on our needs and interests. In the context of integer factorization, we'll demand a very precise approximation to $\\theta$, but in other cases we might be satisfied with a very rough approximation. We'll discuss shortly how the precision we require affects the computational cost of a solution.\n"
msgstr "La formulación anterior del problema de estimación de fase no es específica sobre qué constituye una aproximación de $\\theta$, pero podemos establecer formulaciones más precisas del problema según nuestras necesidades e intereses. En el contexto de la factorización de enteros, requerimos una aproximación muy precisa de $\\theta$, pero en otros casos podría bastarnos una aproximación muy burda. Comentaremos brevemente cómo la precisión requerida afecta al costo computacional de una solución.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Notice that as we go from $\\theta = 0$ toward $\\theta = 1$ in the phase estimation problem, we're going all the way around the unit circle, starting from $e^{2\\pi i \\cdot 0} = 1$ and moving counter-clockwise toward $e^{2\\pi i \\cdot 1} = 1$. That is, when we reach $\\theta = 1$ we're back where we started at $\\theta = 0$. So, as we consider the accuracy of approximations, choices of $\\theta$ near $1$ should be considered as being near $0$. For example, an approximation $\\theta = 0.999$ should be considered as being within $1/1000$ of $\\theta = 0$.\n"
msgstr "Observa: cuando en el problema de estimación de fase pasamos de $\\theta = 0$ hacia $\\theta = 1$, recorremos completamente el círculo unitario, comenzando en $e^{2\\pi i \\cdot 0} = 1$ y moviéndonos en sentido antihorario hacia $e^{2\\pi i \\cdot 1} = 1$. Es decir, cuando alcanzamos $\\theta = 1$, estamos de nuevo donde empezamos con $\\theta = 0$. Así que cuando evaluamos la precisión de las aproximaciones, los valores de $\\theta$ cercanos a $1$ deben considerarse cercanos a $0$. Por ejemplo, una aproximación $\\theta = 0{,}999$ debe considerarse como dentro de $1/1000$ de $\\theta = 0$.\n"
